heading:Minimal Multi-Counter Animated GIF Hit Counter
subheading:Next.js + Vercel + Upstash Redis
paragraph-01:
heading-02:Project structure
codeblock-01:|bin/
├── postflight

pages/
├── _app.tsx
├── api
│   └── counter
│       └── [slug].ts   ← main hit counter
└── index.tsx           ← tiny landing page

public/
├── 0.gif
... 0..9.gif    ← digit sprites (24×32 each)

.env                   ← UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL/_TOKEN
next.config.mjs
package.json
tsconfig.json
vercel.json
readme.md
paragraph-02:Each request to /¥{anything¥}.gif is routed to the [slug] API route which turns the path into a Redis key, increments it with INCR, renders the digits from the public GIF sprites, and returns image/gif.
paragraph-03:
heading-03:Quick deploy to Vercel
paragraph-04:
  1. Install dependencies: npm install
  2. Set environment variables UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL and
     UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN in Vercel project settings
     (copy both from the database page in the Upstash console).
  3. Add your digit GIFs into /public/ as 0.gif .. 9.gif.
  4. Deploy: vercel --prod
paragraph-05:
  Test locally: npm run dev
  Then visit http://localhost:3000/ken/profile-views.gif
paragraph-06:
heading-04:How paths map to Redis keys
table-01:|Requested path                       → Redis key
/ken/profile-views.gif                → ken:profile-views
/ken/commits.gif                      → ken:commits
/project/downloads.gif                → project:downloads
/foo/bar/baz.gif                      → foo:bar:baz
paragraph-07:
heading-05:Environment variables
paragraph-08:|Variable                  Required   Description
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL       yes       Upstash Redis REST URL (https://...upstash.io)
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN     yes       Upstash Redis REST token
paragraph-09:
heading-06:Notes / tradeoffs
paragraph-10:
  • Sprites can have any size, frame count or loop speed: each character keeps
    its own timing, and the transparent background of each sprite is preserved.
    The transparent index is written as white so viewers that flatten GIF
    transparency still show the characters on white, not on a black box.
  • gif-encoder is pure JS; if you can’t use canvas on Vercel, npm i gif-encoder
    and swap the encoder import — both expose the same API in these examples.
  • If you want the public-ingest variant (no API route, just static files +
    middleware), see the extra section at the bottom.
paragraph-11:
  • This is deliberately minimal: no auth, no dashboard, no rate limits,
    no cache layer. Add those later if you need them.
paragraph-12:
